### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

Singmaster's conjecture is a famous open problem in combinatorial number theory. It asks whether there exists a universal constant $C$ such that for every integer $t > 1$, the equation $\binom{n}{k} = t$ (with $1 \le k < n$) has at most $C$ solutions.

The Lean theorem statement is explicitly tagged `@[category research open, AMS 11]`, confirming that this is an open research problem with no known proof. The best known result (due to Kane, 2011) gives a bound of $O(\log t / \log\log t)$ on the number of occurrences, which falls short of the conjectured $O(1)$ bound. No proof of the full conjecture is currently known, so I cannot provide one.
